1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,100
Seen from above, at sunrise on the longest day, every stone throws its shadow the same way — south-west, straight down the monument's axis.

2
00:00:09,583 --> 00:00:12,783
On any other morning, the shadows point elsewhere.

3
00:00:13,542 --> 00:00:22,342
Stand on that line and look back north-east. The first light of the solstice appears inside the slot of the Great Trilithon.

4
00:00:28,583 --> 00:00:37,783
Not every day is marked. At the equinox the Sun sets due west — at the edge of a gap, not inside one.

5
00:00:35,792 --> 00:00:43,392
Six months on, the shadows turn round. In the low winter afternoon they run north-east, up the same axis.

6
00:00:49,875 --> 00:00:57,075
The axis works both ways. From the other side, the midwinter Sun goes down through the same slot.

7
00:00:59,167 --> 00:01:08,767
Four smaller stones mark a rectangle. Its long side points north-west — to where the Moon sets at its northernmost, once every 18.6 years.

